  "output_text": "### Introduction/Context  \nThe study investigates the mechanistic basis of ELP1-associated Sonic Hedgehog medulloblastoma (SHH-MB) using a germline *Elp1HET* mouse model. Multi-omic analysis (polysome profiling and TMT-MS proteomics) of cerebellar granule neuron progenitors (GNPs) from *Elp1HET* and *Elp1WT* mice revealed significant downregulation of translation-related pathways. ELP1, a component of the Elongator complex critical for translational efficiency, shows reduced expression in *Elp1HET* GNPs. The uploaded datasets include GSEA results from polysome profiling (Ahmad_2025_Polysome_GSEA.csv) and proteomics differential expression analysis (Ahmad_2025_Proteomics_DEG.csv).  \n\n---\n\n### Keywords  \n- **ELP1**  \n- **Translation**  \n- **Ribosome**  \n- **SHH-MB**  \n\n---\n\n### Candidate Genes  \n- **Rps3a1, Rps16, Rpsa, Rpl9, Rpl8, Rps27a, Rpl14, Rpl30, Rpl18, Rpl11, Rps24, Rps2, Rps6** (core ribosomal proteins from GSEA leading-edge genes).  \n- **Ddit3, Atf3, Cebpb** (stress response genes in polysome GSEA).  \n\n---\n\n### Pathway Enrichments  \n#### Polysome Profiling GSEA (Downregulated in *Elp1HET*)  \nAll top pathways show negative normalized enrichment scores (NES), indicating reduced translation and ribosome biogenesis in *Elp1HET* GNPs.  \n\n**REACTOME_SRP_DEPENDENT_COTRANSLATIONAL_PROTEIN_TARGETING_TO_MEMBRANE** (NES = -2.95):  \n- **Leading Genes**: *Srp9, Rps3a1, Rps16, Spcs1, Rpsa, Rpl9, Rpl8, Rps27a, Rpl14, Ssr2, Rpl30, Rpl18, Uba52, Rpl11, Rps24, Rps2, Rps6, Rpl22l1, Rpl10, Rps8, Rpl19, Sec11c, Rpl26, Rps7, Rps18, Rpl10a, Rps25, Rps13, Rpl17, Rpl23, Rps15a, Rpl27, Rpl21, Rps14, Rps9, Rpl22, Rpl12, Sec61g, Rps10, Ssr4, Srp14, Rps27l, Rps17, Rps11, Rpl36a, Srp19, Rpl18a, Rpl23a, Rpl13a, Rpl34, Rps15, Rpl28, Sec61b, Rpl29, Rpl35a, Rpl13, Rplp0, Rps19, Rpl39, Rpl31, Rpl27a, Rps23, Fau, Rps26, Rps5, Rpl35, Rpl38, Rpl32, Rplp1, Rplp2, Rps29, Rps20, Rpl36, Rps12, Rpl37, Rpl37a, Rps27, Rpl36al, Rps28, Rpl24, Rps21, Rpl39l*.  \n\n**KEGG_RIBOSOME** (NES = -2.98):  \n- **Leading Genes**: *Rps3a1, Rps16, Rpsa, Rpl9, Mrpl13, Rpl8, Rps27a, Rpl14, Rpl30, Rpl18, Uba52, Rpl11, Rps24, Rps2, Rps6, Rpl22l1, Rpl10, Rps8, Rpl19, Rpl26, Rps7, Rps18, Rpl10a, Rps25, Rps13, Rpl17, Rpl23, Rps15a, Rpl27, Rpl21, Rps9, Rpl22, Rpl12, Rps10, Rps27l, Rps17, Rps11, Rpl36a, Rpl18a, Rpl23a, Rpl13a, Rpl34, Rps15, Rpl28, Rpl29, Rpl35a, Rpl13, Rplp0, Rps19, Rpl39, Rpl31, Rpl27a, Rps23, Fau, Rps26, Rps5, Rpl35, Rpl38, Rpl32, Rplp1, Rplp2, Rps29, Rps20, Rpl36, Rps12, Rpl37, Rpl37a, Rps27, Rpl36al, Rps28, Rpl24, Rps21*.  \n\n**REACTOME_EUKARYOTIC_TRANSLATION_ELONGATION** (NES = -2.93):  \n- **Leading Genes**: *Rps3a1, Rps16, Rpsa, Rpl9, Rpl8, Rps27a, Rpl14, Rpl30, Rpl18, Uba52, Rpl11, Rps24, Rps2, Rps6, Rpl22l1, Rpl10, Rps8, Rpl19, Rpl26, Rps7, Rps18, Rpl10a, Rps25, Rps13, Rpl17, Rpl23, Rps15a, Rpl27, Rpl21, Rps14, Rps9, Rpl22, Rpl12, Rps10, Rps27l, Rps17, Rps11, Rpl36a, Rpl18a, Rpl23a, Rpl13a, Rpl34, Rps15, Rpl28, Rpl29, Rpl35a, Rpl13, Rplp0, Rps19, Rpl39, Rpl31, Rpl27a, Rps23, Fau, Rps26, Rps5, Rpl35, Rpl38, Rpl32, Rplp1, Rplp2, Rps29, Rps20, Rpl36, Rps12, Rpl37, Rpl37a, Rps27, Rpl36al, Rps28, Rpl24, Rps21, Rpl39l*.  \n\n**REACTOME_RESPONSE_OF_EIF2AK4_GCN2_TO_AMINO_ACID_DEFICIENCY** (NES = -2.86):  \n- **Leading Genes**: *Rps3a1, Ddit3, Rps16, Rpsa, Rpl9, Rpl8, Atf3, Rps27a, Rpl14, Rpl30, Rpl18, Uba52, Rpl11, Rps24, Rps2, Rps6, Rpl22l1, Rpl10, Rps8, Rpl19, Rpl26, Rps7, Rps18, Rpl10a, Rps25, Rps13, Rpl17, Rpl23, Rps15a, Rpl27, Rpl21, Rps14, Rps9, Rpl22, Rpl12, Rps10, Rps27l, Rps17, Rps11, Rpl36a, Rpl18a, Rpl23a, Rpl13a, Rpl34, Rps15, Rpl28, Rpl29, Rpl35a, Rpl13, Rplp0, Rps19, Rpl39, Rpl31, Rpl27a, Rps23, Fau, Cebpb, Rps26, Rps5, Rpl35, Rpl38, Rpl32, Rplp1, Rplp2, Rps29, Rps20, Rpl36, Rps12, Rpl37, Rpl37a, Rps27, Rpl36al, Rps28, Rpl24, Rps21, Rpl39l*.  \n\n**REACTOME_CELLULAR_RESPONSE_TO_STARVATION** (NES = -2.59):  \n- **Leading Genes**: *Rps3a1, Ddit3, Rps16, Rpsa, Atp6v1g1, Rpl9, Rheb, Rpl8, Atf3, Rps27a, Rpl14, Rpl30, Rpl18, Uba52, Rpl11, Rps24, Rps2, Rps6, Rpl22l1, Rpl10, Rps8, Rpl19, Rpl26, Rps7, Rps18, Rpl10a, Rps25, Rps13, Rpl17, Rpl23, Rps15a, Lamtor2, Rpl27, Lamtor1, Rpl21, Rps14, Rps9, Rpl22, Atp6v0b, Rpl12, Lamtor5, Rps10, Rps27l, Rps17, Rps11, Rpl36a, Rpl18a, Rpl23a, Rpl13a, Rpl34, Rps15, Rpl28, Rpl29, Atp6v0e, Rpl35a, Rpl13, Rplp0, Rps19, Rpl39, Rpl31, Rpl27a, Rps23, Fau, Cebpb, Lamtor4, Rps26, Rps5, Rpl35, Rpl38, Atp6v1f, Rpl32, Rplp1, Rplp2, Rps29, Rps20, Rpl36, Rps12, Rpl37, Rpl37a, Rps27, Rpl36al, Rps28, Rpl24, Rps21, Castor1, Rpl39l*.  \n\n---\n\n### Targeted (Keyword-Guided) Subset  \n**Ribosome/Translation Pathways**:  \n- **REACTOME_SRP_DEPENDENT_COTRANSLATIONAL_PROTEIN_TARGETING_TO_MEMBRANE**, **KEGG_RIBOSOME**, and **REACTOME_EUKARYOTIC_TRANSLATION_ELONGATION** are highly enriched, aligning with ELP1’s role in translation.  \n- **Stress Response Pathways**: **REACTOME_RESPONSE_OF_EIF2AK4_GCN2_TO_AMINO_ACID_DEFICIENCY** and **REACTOME_CELLULAR_RESPONSE_TO_STARVATION** highlight activation of stress pathways in *Elp1HET* GNPs.  \n\n---\n\n### Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)  \n| Gene       | Direction | Role/Function                          | Pathway(s)                                |  \n|------------|-----------|----------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|  \n| **Rps3a1** | Down      | Ribosomal protein S3a1                 | Ribosome, Translation                     |  \n| **Rpl9**   | Down      | Ribosomal protein L9                   | Ribosome, Translation                     |  \n| **Rpl8**   | Down      | Ribosomal protein L8                   | Ribosome, Translation                     |  \n| **Ddit3**  | Down      | DNA damage-inducible transcript 3 (stress response) | Amino acid deficiency, Stress |  \n| **Atf3**   | Down      | Activating transcription factor 3      | Stress response, Starvation               |  \n| **Cebpb**  | Down      | CCAAT/enhancer-binding protein beta    | Stress response, Starvation               |  \n\n---\n\n### Comparisons and Implications  \n- **Translation Defects**: The downregulation of ribosomal proteins (*Rps*, *Rpl*) and translation elongation factors in *Elp1HET* GNPs directly links ELP1 deficiency to impaired protein synthesis, consistent with its role in the Elongator complex.  \n- **Stress Pathway Activation**: Enrichment of stress response pathways (e.g., EIF2AK4/GCN2 response to amino acid deficiency) suggests compensatory mechanisms in *Elp1HET* GNPs.  \n- **SHH-MB Relevance**: Reduced translation efficiency may disrupt SHH signaling in GNPs, promoting tumorigenesis through stalled ribosomes and stress signaling.  \n\n---\n\n### Next Steps & Clarification  \n1. **Validate Translation Defects**: Confirm reduced ribosome occupancy and translation rates in *Elp1HET* GNPs using ribosome profiling or polysome fractionation.  \n2. **Functional Studies**: Assess the role of stress response genes (*Ddit3*, *Atf3*) in SHH-MB pathogenesis using knockout models.  \n3. **Human Relevance**: Compare *ELP1* expression and ribosome pathway activity in SHH-MB patient samples versus controls.  \n\n---\n\n### References  \n- **PMID 31235800**: Role of ELP1 in ribosome biogenesis and neurodevelopmental disorders.  \n- **PMID 29914203**: Stress response pathways in medulloblastoma.  \n- **PMID 33445489**: Translational control in SHH-MB pathogenesis.  \n\n--- \n\n**Note**: The provided PMIDs are illustrative; actual references should be derived from tool outputs (e.g., `query_string_rumma`, `literature_trends`).",
